# Circle centre: use X, Y

A Viking circle (OData `TypeCode == 1`) has its mosaic centre in `X`, `Y` and its mosaic radius in `Radius`. `MosaicShape` is
`CURVEPOLYGON (CIRCULARSTRING (...))`, a ring of rim points whose **first point is the rightmost rim point** (X + radius), so
taking the first point as the centre draws every circle one radius to the right (this happened in the converter). Select `X,Y`
in every OData query that feeds a circle and use them. Do not derive the centre from the ring (first point, mean, circumcentre).

These tools work in mosaic space only. `Radius` is a mosaic radius: never pair it with `VolumeX`/`VolumeY`, and never use a
volume radius here. Carrying a circle between mosaic and volume space (a scale-aware, five-point mapping) belongs to the Viking
client; see `.cursor/rules/circle-mapping-scale.mdc` in the Viking repo.

The nornir exporter (`segmentationtraining/circles.py`) still takes the circumcentre of three ring points. It agrees with `X`, `Y`
to 0.005 px on RC1 structure 410, but it should move to `X`, `Y` too.
